-- Version 1 → 2: the agent-name columns on issues and comments.
--
-- create_issue and add_comment let an MCP agent stamp the rows it writes with
-- its own name, so the board can show an agent — not a human typing by hand —
-- made them. See the `agent_name` column comments in Issues.sq and Comments.sq
-- for what it means and why it is orthogonal to the author columns.
--
-- Two paths reach that schema, and this file is the one the other cannot cover.
-- A fresh volume runs `CREATE TABLE` from the .sq files and gets the column for
-- free; a volume that already exists at version 1 — Railway's — runs this
-- instead. Without it, the deployed database stays at the old shape and every
-- board query dies on `no such column: agent_name`, which is exactly the failure
-- that shipped before this file existed. See MigrationTest for the guarantee
-- that this lands on the same schema `CREATE TABLE` does.
--
-- ── Why ADD COLUMN, and why that is safe here ────────────────────────────────
--
-- The column is nullable with no default, so SQLite appends it to every existing
-- row as NULL — which is precisely "no agent wrote this", the correct answer for
-- every issue and comment that predates the feature. That is what makes a plain
-- ADD COLUMN right rather than a table rebuild: there is no backfill to compute
-- and no old value to preserve.
--
-- ADD COLUMN can only append, so the column lands physically last here while the
-- .sq CREATE places it mid-table. That difference is invisible to everything
-- that matters: every generated query names its columns explicitly rather than
-- by position, and MigrationTest compares sorted structural facts, so neither the
-- code nor the test treats column order as part of the schema.
ALTER TABLE issues ADD COLUMN agent_name TEXT;
ALTER TABLE comments ADD COLUMN agent_name TEXT;
